This guide creates a complete BakingRL package with a visual, a component, a
service, and a connector.

## Prerequisites

- Node.js 20 or newer.
- npm 10 or newer.
- A local BakingRL app checkout if you want to install and test packages.

## Create A Package

Recommended local setup before npm publication:

```sh
cd BakingRLSDK
npm install
npm install -g .
```

Create a package in a sibling plugin repository:

```sh
cd ../BakingRLPlugins
create-bakingrl-plugin my-package
cd my-package
npm install
```

By default, a generator installed from `BakingRLSDK` uses the SDK from this
checkout through a `file:` dependency.

After editing SDK types or runtime helpers:

```sh
cd ../BakingRLSDK
npm run build
```

If you changed the generator, package helper, templates, or global command
metadata:

```sh
cd ../BakingRLSDK
npm install -g . --force
```

From this repository without global install:

```sh
npm run create -- my-package
cd my-package
npm install
```

From npm, once the scaffolder is published:

```sh
npm create bakingrl-plugin my-package
cd my-package
npm install
```

## Build And Install Locally

```sh
npm run build
npm run pack
npm run install:local
```

`install:local` copies the package into BakingRL's app-data package directory:

- Linux: `$XDG_DATA_HOME/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages` or
  `~/.local/share/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages`
- macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages`
- Windows: `%LOCALAPPDATA%/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages`

Set `BAKINGRL_PACKAGES_DIR` to override the target during development.

## Test In BakingRL

1. Start BakingRL with `npm run tauri dev` from the app repository.
2. Open the Packages tab.
3. Enable your package.
4. Add visual exports to an overlay layout or custom page.
5. Use the layout editor mock events to test event-driven visuals without
   launching Rocket League.

## Development Loop

Use this loop for most plugin work:

```sh
npm run build
npm run check
npm run pack
npm run install:local
```

Then reload packages in BakingRL.
